al communities, leading to the identification of new plastic-degrading bacterial species. For example, Ideonella sakaiensis, Bacillus thermoamylovorans, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa are all thermophilic bacteria that have been shown to degrade plastic and produce biosurfactants. The ability of marine microbiomes to secrete new bioactive secondary metabolites has been demonstrated in earlier investigations. The plastic is eventually degraded by its own microbiome because the plastisphere bacteria uses the plastic components as its food supply to carry out cellular functions and reproduction. The potential contribution of a marine plastisphere microbiome to the production of secondary metabolites remains an open area for investigation. In this study, we computationally investigated the marine microbiome for the production of natural bioactive substances (Wei et al. 2023), (Plastic-Degrading Potential across the Global Microbiome Correlates with Recent Pollution Trends). Terpenes, medicinal peptides, alkaloids, lactones, and steroids are among the natural compounds. These
microbial natural compounds have been documented to be versatile in their actions, such as pest resistance, antifungal, antimicrobial, antiviral, anticancer, and anti-inflammatory properties. Over 23,000 bioactive secondary metabolites are thought to be produced by marine bacteria, and they will continue to be a rich source of bioactive natural products for the ensuing century. The discovery of marine prokaryotic secondary metabolites has drawn increasing attention in recent years, and it has been discovered that these compounds exhibit a higher chemical diversity than secondary metabolites derived from terrestrial microorganisms. To find specific marine microbial secondary metabolites, literature suggested a number of methods, including the use of genome mining techniques that also include metagenome sequencing (Wei et al. 2023). Hence, previous studies do not provide thorough guidelines and open several gaps to investigate and discover a link between plastisphere microbiome to degrade plastic and showing potential for secondary metabolites secretion. Therefore, the current study aimed to investigate the potential of marine plastisphere microbiomes in aiding plastic degradation, mainly through diverse and specialized microbial communities adapted to the marine environment, including thermophilic and biosurfactant-producing bacteria. The study also emphasized the importance of shotgun metagenome sequencing and bioinformatic tools in identifying new plastic-degrading bacterial species, understanding the taxonomic and functional diversity of the plastisphere microbiome, and predicting plastisphere microbial genes responsible for plastic degradation. Ultimately, the future aspect of the current study was to develop more efficient and sustainable plastic waste remediation strategies.
Materials and methods
Sample collection and extraction of environmental DNA (eDNA)
Samples containing polyethylene remains, including seawater, clay, plastic industry effluents, and the effluents contaminated soil, were collected within sterile falcon tubes, aseptically, from a waste dumping site at Clifton Beach, Karachi, Pakistan. As per the manufacturer's protocol, the combined representative environmental sample was subjected to genomic DNA isolation using DNeasy PowerSoil Pro Kit (Qiagen, USA). The quality of extracted DNA was analyzed using lightcycler qPCR and 1% agarose gel electrophoresis while quantified through Quant-IT PicoGreen (Invitrogen).


