Library preparation and metagenome sequencing
High-quality sequencing libraries were prepared according to the manufacturer’s instructions of TruSeq DNA Nano Library Prep Kit (Illumina, Inc., San Diego, CA, USA). The sequencing library was prepared by random fragmentation of the DNA or cDNA sample, followed by 5' and 3' adapter ligation. Following the fragmentation of 100 ng of genomic DNA through adaptive focused acoustic technology (AFA; Covaris), the fragmented DNA was end-repaired to create 5′-phosphorylated, blunt-ended double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) molecules. The DNA size was selected with a bead-based method, adding a single ‘A’ base and ligating the Truseq indexing adapters. The purified libraries were quantified using qPCR according to the qPCR Quantification Protocol Guide (KAPA Library Quantification kits for Illumina Sequencing platforms) and qualified using the Agilent Technologies 2200 TapeStation. (Agilent Technologies). The Illumina Novaseq platform was utilized to perform Whole-Genome Shotgun (WGS) sequencing of TruSeq paired-end libraries to generate 2 × 150 bp paired-end reads. This sequencing was carried out with the Illumina NovaSeq 6000 S2 Reagent Kit (Illumina, San Diego, USA), generating 100 base pair reads through 2 × 150 cycles (George 2021; Skariyachan et al. 2018).
Processing the raw sequencing data
Paired-end read Fastq files were acquired through Shotgun Metagenome Sequencing. Subsequently, Illumina sequence adapters were eliminated, and low-quality bases (< 100 bp) were removed for quality control using Trimmomatic v0.38. Quality and the impact of trimming were assessed via FASTQC v0.11.6. The DNA sequences of trimmed reads were merged and assembled through MEGAHIT v1.2.9, where a minimum K-mer length was set as 83 and a maximum K-mer length was set as 123. To determine where the contigs come from and assess the quality of the assemblies, the trimmed reads of each sample were mapped against the assemblies using bowtie2 v2.2.3. Afterward, the Genome Analysis Toolkit (GATK) v3.4.0 DepthOfCoverage module was used to compute mapping depth stepwise and insert size computation through the Picard CollectInsertSizeMetrics module. The sample contig with an average mapping depth of a value equal to or larger than 3 was stored as a separate FASTA file (Drengenes et al. 2021; Oh et al. 2018; Rölleke et al. 1996; Skariyachan et al. 2018).
