Association of plastisphere microbiome with significant potential bacterial activities
Using the available data on ThermoBase (DiGiacomo et al. 2022; Oliveira et al. 2015), the taxonomic profile of shotgun metagenome was analyzed for the plastisphere microbiome to have the potential for striving in high temperatures. Similarly, the plastisphere microbiome was further analyzed for the potential of degrading synthetic and bioplastics, and biosurfactant production, with the help of BioSurfDB (Oliveira et al. 2015), and PlasticDB (Gambarini et al. 2022). Using AntiSmash database (Blin et al. 2021), secondary metabolites of the plastisphere microbiome were predicted.
Data visualization
Heatmaps and circus-plot were constructed using R package pHeatmap (Kolde 2019), and Circlize (Brors 2014), on RStudio v 4.3.3.2 (Team 2021).
Data availability
The metagenome profile is submitted on NCBI, with the BioSample ID 410658 and BioProject ID PRJNA764827.
Results
Metagenomics sequencing and assembly
A total of 38,078,676 reads were generated by high throughput shotgun metagenome sequencing. Average contig lengths of about 632,427 bp to 200 bp, and approximately 99.52% high-quality read Q20 and Q30 were generated. The highquality read was assembled in 17,312 contigs, an N50 score of 2263, and the minimum length of contigs was 200 bp.
Estimation of microbial community structure
Metagenomic data revealed the presence of 875 distinct bacterial genera in the collected environmental sample, including 3.87% of unidentified microbes, 0.29% of archaea, and 93% of bacterial diversity. The bacterial community included 54% Proteobacteria, 23.9% Firmicutes, 13% Actinobacteria, and 2.1% other bacteria such as Aquificae (0.005%), Bacterioidetes (0.71%), Balneolaeota (0.075%), Chlamydiae (0.01%), Chlorobi (0.005%), Chloroflexi (0.017%), Cyanobacteria (0.075%), Deinococcus (0.063%), Euryarchae (0.288%), Fibrobacteres (0.011%), Fusobacteria (0.040%), Kiritimatiellaeota (0.005%), Lentisphaerae (0.005%), Nitrospinae (0.005%), Planctomycetes (0.092%), Rhodothermaeota (0.005%), Spirochaetes (0.035%), Synergistetes (0.006%), Tenericutes (0.063%), Thaumarchaeota
(0.006%), Thermotogae (0.011%), and Verrucomicrobia (0.017%), as shown in Fig. 1.
Association of plastisphere microbiome with significant potential bacterial activities
Only Pseudomonas showed the capability to thrive under high temperatures, degradation of PHA, synthetic plastic and biosurfactant production. Like Pseudomonas, Streptomyces exhibited thermophilic characteristics and was also involved in the degradation of PHA. Besides, Bacillus, Mycobacterium, Rugeria, Streptomyces, were found to be biosurfactant producers along with having a thermophilic nature. However, Clostridium, Mycobacterium, Enterococcus, Nocardioides, Pseudomonas, Staphylococcus, and Rhodococcus could produce biosurfactants and degrading synthetic plastics. The association of plastisphere microbiome with certain potentials is summarized in Fig. 2.
Overall functional annotation
The predicted genes were translated into respective proteins using the eggnog mapper tool. A total of 2069 proteins were mapped, and 195 proteins did not match with previously identified proteins. As shown in Fig. 3, 1973 proteins were found in the pre-existing protein database, which included 534 proteins with unknown functions, facilitating the idea for conducting extensive research studies to identify their functions. On the other hand, the metagenome contains many proteins involved in transcription, translation, biogenesis, inorganic ion transport and metabolism, amino acid transport and metabolism, carbohydrate transport, and metabolism, energy production and conversion, and other secondary metabolites. Secondary metabolites include the enzymes encompassing plastic degradation and other important clinical and industrial biotechnology-related activities.
Secondary metabolites
The production of secondary metabolites from the plastisphere microbiome was predicted using in-silico analysis. The predicted results of secondary metabolites and their applications are summarized in the Circos plot (Fig. 4). The predicted results reveal that plastisphere microbiome potentially could produce ectoine, lanthipeptides, cyclic lactone autoinducers, transATPKS and NRPS, ranthipeptides, terpenes, and polyketide synthase.
Enrichment of alpha/beta hydrolases
Hydrolases, such as cutinase, depolymerase, esterase, and lipase encoding genes were abundantly found in the plastisphere microbiome. Figure 5 shows alpha and beta


