Discussion
The microbial colonization of plastics established in the marine environment has been well-documented (Wallbank et al. 2022). Still, there is a need for a more thorough study identifying the plastic-degrading microbial communities and their contribution to some prominent attributes encompassing in-silico biosynthesis of industrially and clinically
essential metabolites. This research is the first-ever study of the plastisphere microbiome in Karachi, Pakistan, making it particularly significant. The plastisphere microbiome was investigated in various polyethylene (PE) debris-contained environments, including marine, seashore, and plastic dumping sites. Ideonella sakaiensis, and the species of Marinobacter, Halomonas, Pseudomonas, and Bacillus were abundant in the plastisphere microbiome. This established an understanding of microbial communities of the plastisphere. We have also predicted that the analyzed plastisphere microbiome was involved in degrading plastic and could thrive in high-temperature environments, besides exhibiting the potential to synthesize industrially and clinically important secondary metabolites.
From Pseudomonas, Enterococcus, Mycobacterium, Clostridium, Rhodococcus, Staphylococcus and Streptomyces only Pseudomonas and Streptomyces showed degradation capability for polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA). Pseudomonas were also predicted to degrade synthetic plastic. Moreover, Pseudomonas and Streptomyces showed biosurfactant production, ability to thrive in high temperatures,
