grow at high temperatures and produce numerous oxidase and hydrolase enzymes (Dang et al. 2018). Previous studies showed the degradation of polyethylene (PE), polystyrene foam (PS) and polyethylene terephthalate (PET) by thermophilic microbial consortium, composed of Penicillium raperi, Aspergillus flavus, Penicillium glaucoroseum and Pseudomonas spp. in the presence of biosurfactant (rhamnolipid) (Taghavi et al. 2022). Biological surfactants possess several potential advantages over their chemically manufactured counterparts, including lower toxicity, biodegradability and effectiveness at extreme temperatures, pH and salinity (Midhun et al. 2015). In the investigated plastisphere microbiome, there were 46 bacterial genera that showed involvement in striving and thriving under high temperatures (Fig. 2). Of which, Pseudomonas and Streptomyces were found to be PHA degraders; and Mycobacterium and Pseudomonas were synthetic plastic degraders. Moreover, Bacillus, Mycobacterium, Pseudomonas, Rugeria, and Streptococcus were found
