Bacterial ectoin synthesis increases under hypo- or hyperosmotic stress (Di Costanzo et al. 2021; Kauth and Trusova 2022). Moving to the lanthipeptide secretion, these fascinating compounds exhibit a broad spectrum of antimicrobial activities, encompassing antibacterial and antifungal functions and antiviral, antinociceptive, antiallodynic, and morphogenetic properties. Lanthipeptides identified so far are made mainly by Gram-positive bacteria, such as pinensins, which are fungicidal and found in bactericides (Caetano et al. 2020; Castro et al. 2021). Terpenes, polyketides, nonribosomal peptides, and RiPPs (ribosomally produced and post-translationally modified peptides) are the most common bioactive secondary metabolites in bacteria that include the class Halobacteria, also known as haloarchaea. Because they are halophiles, haloarchaea can only exist in a small range of ambient salt concentrations. They are the dominant prokaryotic species in hypersaline, thalassohaline, and athalassohaline habitats (such as marine settings). They have also been thought to make ideal candidates for life on Mars since they can flourish in stromatolites and ancient halite (Najjari et al. 2015; Oren 2014; Yu et al. 2013). Interestingly, natural terpenes such as linalool (L), geraniol (G), and geranyl acetate (GA) have been reported for synthesizing poly(3-hydroxybutyrate), which is a kind of bioplastic (Mangeon et al. 2018; Turco et al. 2020). Actinobacteria are a treasure trove of lanthipeptides, as revealed by genome sequencing, which has shown an abundance of lanthipeptide biosynthetic gene clusters. The hexapeptide isolated from Streptomyces rochei Sal35, the roseocin isolated from Streptomyces roseosporus, the mathermycin isolated from Marinactinospora thermotolerans, the cebulantin isolated from Saccharopolyspora cebuensis, the microbisporicin isolated from Microbispora corallina, and others has all been reported in previous studies (Chen et al. 2017; Fernández-Martínez et al. 2015; Gomes et al. 2017; Li et al. 2021; Moon et al. 2019; Singh et al. 2020; Uritskiy et al. 2019; Vikeli et al. 2020; Walker et al. 2020; Xu et al. 2020).
Conclusion
The marine plastisphere microbiome, sourced from diverse environments encompassing brine water with microplastics, plastic waste disposal sites, and clay deposits at coastal zones, represents a repository of significant bioactive compounds. In addition to its pivotal role in mitigating plastic pollution through bioremediation, this microbiome exhibits substantial potential for biosurfactant production, particularly rhamnolipids, which have demonstrated remarkable efficacy in plastic degradation. Furthermore, this plastisphere microbiome showcases a notable capacity for the biosynthesis of secondary metabolites, a critical facet in
advancing pharmaceutical and enzyme systems, offering solutions that streamline clinical and industrial processes. This study firmly establishes a robust connection between the marine plastisphere microbiome and its ability to produce industrially and clinically relevant bioactive compounds, all while effectively addressing the pervasive issue of plastic pollution. Notably, Pseudomonas emerges as a critical player among these microorganisms’ demonstrating involvement in plastic degradation, biosurfactant production, and exhibiting thermophilic characteristics. Consequently, this study pioneers a forward-looking approach towards comprehensively investigating the functions and attributes of these secondary metabolites to harness their potential in a targeted and constructive manner. More research should be conducted to further validate these results.
